**Never back down! Never what ?!**

### Intro 

That was what I had to chant when tackling this section of the project - and I barely started. Heap management was proving more difficult then I hoped - multiple heaps, `double linked lists` for `segment` navigation, `heap entries` per segment - and `uncommitted ranges` ,`VAIL` <- Virtual Allocation entries,  `decoding` the heap entries and I am not even talking about whole other "mode" of operation  -> `segment heap`

In this post we are going to tackle only the classic NT HEAP  with basic `_HEAP_ENTRY` walk . 
The structure, the structure and again the structure ...  Before we can take even a single step we have to familiarize ourselves with the internal structure of `_HEAP` 

At first I was relying solely on [VERGILIUS](https://www.vergiliusproject.com/) Project that documents the internal structures across time and architectures .  

Bellow is a very  ( and i repeat a very simplistic depiction of `HEAP` structure )

```
    _HEAP                                       
┌────────────────────────────────────────┐      
│ ┌────────────────────────────────────┐ │      
│ │      _HEAP_SEGMENT                 │ │      
│ │         ┌─────────────────────────┐│ │      
│ │    0x0  │ _HEAP_ENTRY             ││ │      
│ │         │ 0x18    SegmentListEntry││ │      
│ │         │ 0x40    FirstEntry      │◄─┼─────┐
│ │         │ 0x48    LastValidEntry  ││ │     │
│ │         │                         ││ │     │
│ │         └─────────────────────────┘│ │     │
│ └────────────────────────────────────┘ │     │
│                                        │     │
│                                        │     │
│  0x80 _HEAP_ENTRY Encoding             │     │
│                                        │     │
│  0x110            VirtualAllocdBlocks  │     │
│  0x120            SegmentList    ──────┼─────┘
│                                        │      
│                                        │      
│                                        │         
│                                        │      
│                                        │      
└────────────────────────────────────────┘      
```


### Talking the talk

I mentioned in my previous post that `Heap32First/Next` wont be sufficient. Microsoft them selves state that : *Walking the heap with **Heap32First** and **Heap32Next** is inefficient, particularly for large heaps. Use [HeapWalk](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/desktop/api/heapapi/nf-heapapi-heapwalk) instead.* 

The `HeapWalk` is only for a calling process so thats a no go. The Heap32First/Next is what I implemented anyway. From what I read ( and in some sense experienced my self ) the difference between the manual walk and `ToolHelp` approach  boils down to evidence depth and suitability. 
While toolHelp approach is good for debugging lets say it often quoted as *surface-level inventory* .

Manually walking the `_HEAP_ENTRIES` lends us into territory of [structured heap forensics](https://webdiis.unizar.es/~ricardo/files/slides/academic/slides-DFRWS-EU-26.pdf#1#1#1) which points out that the traditional flat memory view ( signature based carving etc..) lacks the structural context needed for `Heap spraying` attacks `Use-after-free` exploits and `Heap Feng shui` manipulation . Which all sounds nice and fancy but lets actually  take a look and ( try to ) walk the heap manually 
### Walking the walk

Couple of things before we take our first step. This is not a comprehensive HEAP entry walk - not even close -  for now I am walking the Back-End  NT HEAP - i am completely skipping `LFH` buckets , I have not implemented newer `Segment Heap` and `VirtualAllocdBlocks` 

In future I hope I get to cover those topics too but for now we can demystify the elementary `_HEAP_ENTRY` walk. 

The pain point in this is that all of the documentation is - lets say verry volatile. Its not very well documented in the first place and what is documented changes over time a build releases so things break.  For my research I workd with latest win 10 so these are the offsets ( [22H2 (2022 Update, Vibranium R5)](https://www.vergiliusproject.com/kernels/x64/windows-10/22h2))  I was working with.

The basic logic of walking and decoding `_HEAP_ENTRY` is to get all `_HEAP`s pointers from `PEB.ProcessHeaps` (heap address + 0xf0) for each HEAP pointer get all segments by walking the  heap segment double linked list   form list head `flink`  that points to first segment of the heap which is embedded as segment entry at the start of the heap. 

So basically the `SegmentList` -> `flink` points to `LIST_ENTRY` of the first segment - which happens to be the `base of the heap` + `0x18` . We can continue this loop following the `flink` of  each subsequent `HEAP_SEGMENT` after which we have to  subtract the `0x18` from the address to get to the root of the entry so we can map the struct cleanly. 

One things that took me longer than it should have was definitely the termination of the loop - How do i know that i am at the start of the loop ? I could have just store the visited addresses and check each time I followed the `flink` but I decided that following the logic that was intended would be "more" fun ... well spoiler alert it was not I got stuck on figuring out what the `list head`  was . From what I read it should have been the `SegmentList` entry in `HEAP`   which is `LIST_ENTRY` so I thought that the first `flink` would be also the last. Well it was not and to spare you the hassle its the `SegmentList` itself - so address to terminate the loop would be `HEAP` base + `0x120` 

So we have the `HEAP`s the `HEAP_SEGMENT`s  only thing left if is to collect the `HEAP_ENTRY`/ies . But as this post is running little longer then I would like we will tackle this in the next one! Take care and stay curious !
